<h2>Distance Around Shapes — perimeter and units</h2>
<p>Perimeter is the <strong>length around the boundary</strong>, not its area. Start with the original paperclip photograph: how many equal clips would cover every side? A larger clip means fewer are needed.</p>
<p><a href="https://cms-assets.illustrativemathematics.org/rsbxrdpc5vvyqhoctz4px93kkojy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Original student task PDF (3 pages)</a> and <a href="https://cms-assets.illustrativemathematics.org/gs8uwncrcr0ksgmfdp0s2k3fswuy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Original blackline masters ZIP — teacher paperclip A–D</a>. Source figures stay at the publisher and have not been copied.</p>
<table class="source-chart"><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>How to measure</th><th>Teacher's source evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th>Physical shapes A–D</th><td>1¼-inch clips, or scaled copy for 1-inch clips</td><td>A 13, B 9, C 12, D 14; D most</td></tr>
<tr><th>Student PDF grid figures A and B</th><td>Count dot-to-dot intervals on EVERY boundary side</td><td>A 22, concave B 26 grid units</td></tr>
<tr><th>Student PDF tick figures C, D, E</th><td>Count marked edge intervals</td><td>C 33, D 32, E 30 tick units</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>Do not compare A's 22 grid units directly to C's 33 printed tick units. The original teacher explains that those unit lengths are not the same. Count the inward boundary of shape B, not only its bounding rectangle.</p>
